B = [
('Jingdezhen Imperial Kilns', 'East Asia', 'Ming-Qing dynasties', 'ceramics', 'In the style of Jingdezhen Imperial Kilns, thin-walled white porcelain with a glassy, faintly bluish transparent glaze, painted under the glaze in rich cobalt blue. Motifs include coiling lotus scrolls, dragons chasing flaming pearls, fish and water weed, peony sprays and bands of ruyi clouds and waves, repeated in tight concentric bands around vases, bowls, stem cups and moon flasks. Cobalt shades range from pale silvery blue to deep inky blue with dark pooled spots. Forms are elegant and balanced, with rounded shoulders, narrow foot rings and crisp rims. Overglaze enamel variants add coral red, apple green, yellow and turquoise outlined in fine black, and a small six-character mark in a blue double ring sits on the base.'),
('Longquan Celadon Kilns', 'East Asia', 'Song dynasty', 'ceramics', 'In the style of Longquan Celadon Kilns, thick-glazed stoneware in a soft, jade-like green, from pale plum and sea green to deep leaf green, with a lustrous, unctuous, slightly translucent finish that pools thicker in recesses. Forms are restrained and sculptural, such as lotus-petal bowls, plum vases with swelling shoulders, tripod incense burners and fish-handled vases. Decoration is minimal, with carved or moulded lotus petals, incised combed lines and sometimes a fine crackle network. Rims fade lighter where the glaze thins, and the unglazed foot ring burns to russet brown. The mood is quiet, scholarly and understated, like polished river stone.'),
('Ando Cloisonne', 'East Asia', 'Meiji-Showa 1880s-1930s', 'decorative arts', 'In the style of Ando Cloisonne, Japanese cloisonne vases, boxes and plates with smooth, glossy enamel fields held by hair-thin silver or brass wires. Colours are deep and jewel-like, including midnight blue, black, wisteria violet, jade green, ivory and warm gold, with a mirror finish and no visible wire thickness. Subjects are delicate birds, hydrangea, wisteria, irises, chrysanthemums, butterflies and tiny geometric brocade borders, often floating on a plain black or blue ground with generous empty space. Wireless techniques create soft gradients in petals and feathers. Vessels are slender, with narrow necks, flared mouths and silver rims, and are finished with gilt metal collars.'),
('Kutani Ware', 'East Asia', 'Edo-present', 'ceramics', 'In the style of Kutani Ware, porcelain painted with dense, opaque overglaze enamels in five dark, saturated colours, namely deep green, Prussian blue, yellow, purple and aubergine red. Designs cover nearly the whole surface with bold landscapes, birds, flowers, geometric diaper patterns and fine black outlines. A second manner uses a bright red body painting with gold, carrying tiny figures and ornate panels in minute detail. Green is often laid on thickly as a background with a heavy, jewel-like gloss. Shapes include plates, sake cups, jars and lidded bowls, with a rich, theatrical, almost tapestry-like richness and a thick bright glaze.'),
('Arita Imari Kilns', 'East Asia', '17th-19th century', 'ceramics', 'In the style of Arita Imari Kilns, white porcelain decorated in a crowded palette of underglaze cobalt blue, iron red and gold. Panels of chrysanthemums, peonies, pine, bamboo, cranes and phoenix are packed into fan, circle and brocade-pattern reserves that fill every part of the plate, bowl or jar. Black outlines, lacy borders, flaring scalloped rims and lobed forms are typical. Red and blue dominate while green and black add accents, and gilding glows on petals and borders. Lidded jars and large bottles are tall and rounded, giving an opulent, ornate and festive effect.'),
('Nambu Tekki', 'East Asia', 'Edo-present', 'metalwork', 'In the style of Nambu Tekki, cast-iron kettles and teapots with a heavy, squat, rounded body, a dark matte black or rust-brown finish and a lacquered iron-oxide surface. The skin is covered in rows of raised small round studs called arare, or in smooth walls with fine texture. Details are a flat lid with a small knob in brass or iron, a swinging bail handle, a short spout, a firm foot and a thick lip. The surface looks like old weathered metal with a slight sheen, and a few forms are decorated with raised pine, plum or wave motifs. Tea-ceremony restraint, sturdiness and quiet weight define them.'),
('Wajima-nuri Lacquer Houses', 'East Asia', 'Edo-present', 'lacquerware', 'In the style of Wajima-nuri Lacquer Houses, bowls, trays, boxes and chopsticks with thick, glassy, flawless coats of black or deep vermilion urushi lacquer polished to a mirror depth. Surfaces are smooth, with a warm glow, and feel substantial. The foundation shows only in sturdy, rounded edges and neat foot rings. Decoration is restrained, with fine gold maki-e flowers, cranes and grasses, or inlaid chin-kin lines carved into the lacquer and filled with gold leaf. Red interiors contrast with black exteriors. The overall mood is formal, luxurious and calm, with crisp, perfectly even shapes.'),
('Kamakura-bori', 'East Asia', 'Kamakura-present', 'woodcraft', 'In the style of Kamakura-bori, carved wooden trays, bowls, boxes and mirrors cut from katsura or ginkgo and finished in red-brown or black lacquer. Carving is bold and shallow, using gouge cuts to form camellia flowers, peonies, cherry blossoms, leaves and waves, with rounded petal edges and flat bevelled leaf surfaces. The lacquer is slightly matte, built from a vermilion or cinnabar layer over black that wears at the high points to expose a warm brown underlayer. Forms are simple discs, rounded rectangles and lobed trays with deep carved rims. The look is folk-craft, sculptural and handmade, with visible tool marks.'),
('Iznik Ceramic Workshops', 'Middle East', '16th century', 'ceramics', 'In the style of Iznik Ceramic Workshops, white quartz-frit ceramic painted under a brilliant clear glaze with vivid fresh colour. The characteristic palette is cobalt blue, turquoise, emerald green and a thick raised tomato-red, drawn crisply with black outlines on a bright white ground. Patterns are tulips, carnations, hyacinths, rosebuds, saz leaves, curling feathery leaves, and cloud bands arranged symmetrically in flowing arabesques. Tiles, plates, jugs and mosque lamps are glossy, with a crisp, fresh look and rhythmic, balanced repetition. Large wall panels form garden-like compositions around arches and borders.'),
('Zildjian Cymbal Foundry', 'Middle East', '17th century-present', 'musical instruments', 'In the style of Zildjian Cymbal Foundry, hand-hammered bronze cymbals with a bright warm gold sheen, circular and dished with a raised central bell. The surface shows concentric lathing grooves and dense dimpled hammer marks, with darker, burnished tones in the recesses and polished highlights on the ridges. Traditional versions look dark, brown and dull gold while newer versions are brilliantly polished and mirror bright. A small hole in the bell takes the strap, edges are thin and slightly wavy, and a printed stamp with an ornate logo, in black or gold ink, sits near the bell. Variations include flat rides, crash cymbals and hi-hats.'),
('Tabriz Carpet Workshops', 'Middle East', '16th-20th century', 'textiles', 'In the style of Tabriz Carpet Workshops, finely knotted wool and silk rugs with a central medallion on an open or dense field, framed by a wide main border and several narrow guard borders. Colours are rich and layered, with ivory, deep madder red, indigo, navy, soft turquoise and gold. Motifs include curling vines, palmettes, hunting scenes, trees of life, vases of flowers and cartouches, drawn in very precise, curved lines. The pile is short and velvety, with sharply defined details, strict symmetry and a spacious, courtly composition. Corner spandrels echo the medallion shape, and fringes are neat and white.'),
('Kashan Carpet Workshops', 'Middle East', '19th-20th century', 'textiles', 'In the style of Kashan Carpet Workshops, closely knotted wool and silk carpets in a luxurious, deep colour scheme dominated by claret and ruby red, midnight blue and ivory, with a velvet-soft short pile. A large lobed central medallion with pendants floats on a field of dense scrolling vines, palmettes and arabesque blossoms, framed by a wide border with repeating floral cartouches. Fine detail, perfect symmetry and a slightly glossy sheen make them formal and elegant. Corners repeat quarter medallions, and tiny outlines in ivory define the stems and leaves.'),
('Fes Zellij Workshops', 'Africa', 'Marinid-present', 'tilework', 'In the style of Fes Zellij Workshops, mosaic panels of hand-cut glazed clay tiles inlaid into an interlocking geometric puzzle. Stars with eight, twelve or sixteen points radiate from the centre, with kite, hexagon and chevron pieces in vibrant glossy colours, namely cobalt blue, emerald green, saffron yellow, white, black, deep red-brown and turquoise. Fountains, walls and floors carry the repeating tessellations, and edges are crisp with thin white grout lines. Borders carry calligraphic bands or carved plaster, and surfaces sit below carved cedar and stucco. The result is a precise, endlessly repeating, jewel-like pattern.'),
('Venini Murano Glass', 'Europe', '1940s-50s', 'glass', 'In the style of Venini Murano Glass, hand-blown Venetian art glass in elegant simple shapes with thin walls and flowing asymmetric curves. Colours are translucent and luminous, including emerald green, amber, ruby, aquamarine, lilac and opaque milk white, sometimes layered with a clear casing. Techniques create fused patches of colour like woven fabric, delicate lace threads, vertical ribbons, bubbled inclusions and acid-etched satin surfaces. Vases, bowls, bottles and chandeliers feel light, sculptural and playful, with clean lines inspired by mid-century modernism. Many pieces are polished at the rim and carry a small etched mark.'),
('Fortuny Textiles', 'Europe', 'Early 20th century', 'textiles', 'In the style of Fortuny Textiles, printed velvet and cotton furnishing fabrics with densely stamped metallic pigment in gold and silver on deep, mottled backgrounds. Colours are rich and muted, including aubergine, indigo, burnt sienna, olive, ochre, antique rose and dusty teal, with a hazy, layered, hand-printed look. Patterns are Renaissance pomegranates, Islamic arabesques, Cretan and Persian stylised animals, ogee lattices and medallions repeating in tight rows. The surface has a faint shimmer from the metal ink, a soft aged glow and a velvety hand. Silk pleated gowns and lamps with a painted parchment shade complete the rich, theatrical, exotic mood.'),
('Gobelins Manufactory', 'Europe', '17th-18th century', 'textiles', 'In the style of Gobelins Manufactory, huge woven wool and silk wall tapestries that look like paintings, with dense hatching of finely graded coloured threads. Compositions are grand and theatrical, showing mythological and historical scenes, hunting parties, hanging garlands and architectural frames with heavy woven borders of acanthus, laurel, fruit, shields and gilt-looking scrollwork. Palette is deep and slightly aged, with crimson, royal blue, forest green, ochre, gold and warm brown, plus pale highlights. Figures are idealised and baroque in pose with billowing drapery and dramatic clouds, and the weave is tight, flat and smooth.'),
('Aubusson Tapestry Workshops', 'Europe', '17th-19th century', 'textiles', 'In the style of Aubusson Tapestry Workshops, flat-woven wool and silk tapestries and rugs with soft, pastel-leaning colour. The palette uses faded rose pink, powder blue, sage green, cream, pale gold and muted terracotta. Designs include verdure scenes of leafy forests with birds, large acanthus leaves, floral bouquets, pastoral figures and romantic medallions framed by woven ribbons, garlands and scrolling cartouches. The weave is flat with no pile and a slightly chalky, dusty, antique hand. Compositions feel airy, graceful, curved and symmetrical, in a Rococo and Neoclassical manner, with delicate cream borders.'),
('Beauvais Tapestry Manufactory', 'Europe', '18th century', 'textiles', 'In the style of Beauvais Tapestry Manufactory, woven tapestries and upholstery panels with a playful, pastoral Rococo character. Scenes show gallant shepherds, children at play, garden romances, exotic animals and fables, rendered in soft, sweet colours such as pink, cream, sky blue, pale green and gold with brown outlines. Frames of woven flowers, ribbons, scallops and C-shaped scrolls surround chair backs, screens and sofas. The weave is fine and smooth and the colours glow like a painted pastel. Mood is light, romantic and decorative, with plump forms and flowing curves.'),
('Savonnerie Manufactory', 'Europe', '17th-18th century', 'textiles', 'In the style of Savonnerie Manufactory, thick, plush hand-knotted wool pile carpets with a velvet surface and a sculptural, monumental pattern. Layouts are formal and symmetrical, with large acanthus scrolls, floral bouquets, shells, cornucopia, garlands and architectural cartouches on a dark ground in deep blue, black, brown or crimson. Motifs are shaded to look three-dimensional, with ivory, gold, rose, sage green and yellow highlights. Borders are wide, with trompe-l oeil mouldings and corners holding fruit and flowers. The overall feeling is that of a royal palace floor, sumptuous, ordered and heavy.'),
('Sevres Porcelain Manufactory', 'Europe', '18th century', 'ceramics', 'In the style of Sevres Porcelain Manufactory, soft-paste and hard-paste porcelain with deep saturated ground colours, namely a rich royal blue, turquoise, a delicate rose pink and apple green, all heavily gilded. Reserved panels on vases, tea services and plates hold finely painted cherubs, pastoral figures, birds and flower bouquets in naturalistic colours. Gilt borders, scrolls, beading and lacy foliage trim every edge, and handles are sculpted into curving branches or flower forms. Forms are swelling, ornate and Rococo, with a glossy, perfectly smooth glaze. Biscuit figurines remain matte white, with silky, snow-like texture.'),
('Royal Delft', 'Europe', '17th century-present', 'ceramics', 'In the style of Royal Delft, tin-glazed earthenware with a creamy white ground and a vivid cobalt blue painted decoration. Scenes include windmills, canals, sailing ships, tulips, villagers, birds and landscapes drawn with fine brushwork and tonal shading. Plates, tiles, tall tulip vases and vases with pyramids of spouts feature curling scrollwork, floral borders and small figures. The glaze is shiny, slightly bluish-white and pooled in places, and the blue ranges from pale to near-black. Tile pictures in grids repeat simple blue motifs in corner spandrels. The mood is crisp, domestic, nostalgic and traditionally Dutch.'),
('Whitefriars Glass', 'Europe', '1960s-70s', 'glass', 'In the style of Whitefriars Glass, heavy, thick-walled studio-style glass with bold sculptural shapes and a textured surface. Famous items include bark-textured vases with irregular tree-like ridges, chunky bubbles and cased layers of strong colour. The palette is deep and earthy, with tangerine orange, meadow green, kingfisher blue, aubergine purple, ruby red, amber and smoky grey. Forms are cylindrical, tapered, faceted or swirled, with heavy bases and slight asymmetry. Moulded ribbed pattern and dimpled decoration create a strong tactile, glowing mid-century impression. Surfaces are glossy, with a deep inner colour.'),
('Steuben Glass', 'North America', '1930s-50s', 'glass', 'In the style of Steuben Glass, crystal clear, flawless, heavy lead-free optical glass vases, bowls and sculptures with a pure glass transparency and thick, polished walls. Shapes are clean, simple and modernist, with crisp curves, tapered columns, deep facets and bowls resting on thick bases. Engraved or cut decoration is minimal, such as thin copper-wheel engraved animals, fish and plants, or sharp geometric cuts that refract light. Colour appears rarely, in subtle tinted tones such as pale gold or aqua. The mood is cool, luminous, precise and quiet, like a block of ice shaped by hand.'),
('Gorham Silver', 'North America', '1880s-1900s', 'silverware', 'In the style of Gorham Silver, heavy sterling tableware, tea sets and trophies in a lavish late-Victorian and Art Nouveau manner. Surfaces are hand-hammered or chased, with bright polished highlights and darker oxidised recesses. Decoration includes embossed flowers, lily pads, whiplash curves, dragonflies, Japanese-inspired motifs, mixed metal inlays of copper and brass, and flowing organic handles. Teapots and bowls are rounded, with applied leaves and figural finials and ebonised or ivory-coloured insulators. Pieces have a rich, sculptural, ornamental feel and a characteristic martelled texture. The marks are small stamped symbols on the base.'),
('Rookwood Pottery', 'North America', '1880s-1920s', 'ceramics', 'In the style of Rookwood Pottery, glazed art pottery vases with a high-gloss, dripping or smooth glaze and painted decoration. The signature standard glaze has dark backgrounds blending from brown to amber, orange and olive green, with painted flowers, leaves, fruit and animals, such as nasturtiums or pansies, in a naturalistic, softly blurred manner. Later styles include matte glazes in soft green, blue and pink, as well as Art Deco forms with geometric and stylised flowers. Forms are rounded, with simple lines and a mix of tall vases and bowls. Marks on the base include a stamped flame-ringed emblem.'),
('Roycroft Arts and Crafts Community', 'North America', 'Early 20th century', 'decorative arts', 'In the style of Roycroft Arts and Crafts Community, rectilinear oak furniture, hand-hammered copper and books made in a plain handmade manner. Furniture has square, sturdy legs, exposed through-tenons, flat slats and a warm medium-brown fumed finish, with visible grain. Copper bowls, lamps and bookends show hammer marks, rivets, strap hinges and a deep brown or warm penny-red patina. Books use thick handmade paper, rubricated initials, woodcut-like borders, heavy black type and limp suede or vellum covers with a stamped orb and cross emblem. The mood is simple, honest, rustic and monastic.'),
('Ntonso Adinkra Cloth Makers', 'Africa', 'Traditional-present', 'textiles', 'In the style of Ntonso Adinkra Cloth Makers, handwoven cotton cloth stamped with symbols using carved calabash stamps and a dark, glossy tree-bark dye. Rows of small, bold black motifs, such as concentric circles, spirals, crosses, ladders, hearts and stylised plants, repeat inside a grid divided by hand-drawn comb lines. Backgrounds are dyed deep red, brown, indigo, black or left natural cream, and mourning cloths are very dark. Symbols are slightly irregular, with inky edges, giving a handmade rhythm and graphic quality. The cloth is wrapped around the body like a toga and has stitched strips joined together.'),
('Bonwire Kente Weavers', 'Africa', 'Traditional-present', 'textiles', 'In the style of Bonwire Kente Weavers, narrow strips of silk and cotton woven on a horizontal loom and sewn edge to edge into a large cloth of bold, vivid geometric blocks. Colours are saturated gold yellow, kelly green, scarlet red, royal blue and black, with white accents. Patterns are checkerboards, stepped zigzags, diamonds, stripes, bars and small supplementary-weft motifs that look embroidered, floating over plain bands. Strips alternate between dense pattern and plain colour, with a strong rhythmic structure and no empty space. The cloth is thick, glossy and richly patterned, with horizontal and vertical offsets between the strips.'),
('Hudson Bay Company Point Blankets', 'North America', '18th-20th century', 'textiles', 'In the style of Hudson Bay Company Point Blankets, thick, heavy wool blankets in a creamy off-white field with four horizontal stripes near each edge, in green, red, yellow and indigo blue. The stripes are bold, flat and evenly spaced, with the green and red dominating, and small black tick marks woven into one edge to show size. The cloth has a dense napped, felted surface that feels rustic, warm and hearty, with finished edges and no further decoration. Blankets are sold folded with a woven tag. Variants offer a sturdy single stripe, but the classic four-stripe look suggests trading posts, cabins and snow.'),
('Ganado Trading Post Navajo Weavings', 'North America', '1900s-1930s', 'textiles', 'In the style of Ganado Trading Post Navajo Weavings, handwoven wool rugs with a deep Ganado red ground, black and white, and grey borders. A bold central diamond, often with stepped serrated edges or a cross motif, is placed in the centre, with smaller diamonds or hooks echoing in corners. Colours are earthy and limited, namely brick red, charcoal black, ivory and soft grey, with strong contrast. The border is a single frame with notches or crosses, and edges are neat. Weave is tight and flat with a slight handspun irregularity. Geometry is crisp, rectilinear and strongly symmetrical, with a warm, desert look.'),
('Aldine Press', 'Europe', '1490s-1510s', 'publishing', 'In the style of Aldine Press, small portable books with a clean, balanced page, set in a crisp roman type with open, round letters and a slanted, narrow italic with joined strokes. Pages are uncluttered, with generous margins, justified text, simple initial capitals and almost no illustration. Title pages carry a small printer mark of a dolphin coiled around an anchor in black ink on cream paper. Bindings are soft vellum or brown leather with blind tooling, gilt edges and ties. The look is elegant, humanist, scholarly and compact, with an even, quiet, evenly inked colour on laid paper.'),
('Plantin Press', 'Europe', '16th century', 'publishing', 'In the style of Plantin Press, dense, sturdy books of the Renaissance with well-spaced roman typefaces, italic accents and elaborate woodcut or copper-engraved title pages. Title pages are architectural, with columns, pediments, allegorical figures, garlands, cartouches and a printer emblem of a hand holding a compass. Pages show black ink on warm cream laid paper, with decorative headpieces, ornamented initials and neat marginal notes. Multi-language Bible volumes use columns in several scripts, symmetrical and orderly. Bindings are in tooled calf or vellum. The look is rich, rational, ornate and humanist.'),
('Kelmscott Press', 'Europe', '1890s', 'publishing', 'In the style of Kelmscott Press, dense Gothic-revival books printed in heavy black blackletter type on thick handmade cream paper, with red ink for headings and marginal notes. Each page is framed by luxuriant woodcut borders of winding acanthus, vines, flowers, leaves and fruit in white on black, interlaced thickly. Large decorative initials in foliage set against dark ground open each chapter. Text blocks are tightly justified, with minimal leading and wide margins, giving a dark and ornate texture. Bindings use limp vellum with silk ties or half holland with boards. The mood is medieval, rich and romantic.'),
('Doves Press', 'Europe', '1900s-1910s', 'publishing', 'In the style of Doves Press, austere and elegant books with no illustration and no border. Pages are printed in a single classic roman type in rich black ink on thick cream handmade paper, with even word spacing, generous margins and a perfectly balanced column. Only coloured initials in red or blue, drawn with flowing calligraphic flourishes, add ornament, and chapter openings use large letters with simple lines. Title pages are plain with tidy letter spacing. Bindings are tooled with a simple gilt line pattern on limp vellum or morocco. The mood is pure, calm, disciplined and typographic.'),
('Elzevir Press', 'Europe', '17th century', 'publishing', 'In the style of Elzevir Press, tiny pocket-sized duodecimo books with neat, crisp roman and italic text set in narrow columns on small pages. The title page has a copper-engraved frame or a small printer device of a tree wound with a vine and an old man beside it. Typography is compact and clear, with compact page numbers, ornamental headpieces of printers flowers and small decorated initials. Paper is white to cream, and bindings are plain vellum or brown calf with gilt tooling and small marbled endpapers. The mood is compact, practical, tidy and scholarly.'),
('Monotype Corporation', 'Europe', '1920s-30s', 'typography', 'In the style of Monotype Corporation, printed matter and specimen sheets showing carefully revived classical roman typefaces and crisp new classics. Text is set in fine serif with high contrast, bracketed serifs, balanced proportions and even colour, along with a few geometric sans-serif and slab designs. Specimen pages are neat, black ink on cream paper, with rows of alphabets in graduated sizes, small numbers and ornamental rules. Layouts use centred headings, small caps, letter-spaced capitals and discreet fleurons. The overall look is orderly, refined, classical and typographic, in the manner of a well-printed British book or newspaper.'),
('Letraset', 'Europe', '1960s-70s', 'graphics', 'In the style of Letraset, dry-transfer lettering sheets with rows of black or coloured typefaces printed on translucent sheets, with a waxy, slightly cracked appearance when rubbed down. Typeface samples are bold, expressive 1960s and 70s display faces, including chunky rounded letters, psychedelic swirls, inline shadows, stencil cuts, art-nouveau revival and space-age geometric forms. Sheets are laid out in a neat grid with tiny alphabets and punctuation, along with registration marks and a blue instruction grid. Words applied to paper are slightly uneven, with tiny gaps and imperfect baseline. The mood is cut-and-paste, punk poster and retro graphic design.'),
('Rotring', 'Europe', '1950s-80s', 'writing instruments', 'In the style of Rotring, precision technical drawing pens and mechanical pencils with a slim, hexagonal or round metal barrel in matte black or silver, with a knurled grip and a red ring around the top. The shape is industrial and tool-like, with a long conical metal nose, a retractable sleeve, a clip and tiny printed line-width numbers in white on the barrel. Colours are black, brushed chrome and a vivid red accent. Technical pens use colour-coded end caps and ink cartridges. Finishes are crisp and engineered, and the mood is draughtsman, architect studio and exacting accuracy.'),
('Staedtler', 'Europe', '1900s-present', 'stationery', 'In the style of Staedtler, drawing pencils and erasers in a distinctive scheme. Wooden pencils have a hexagonal body lacquered in bright blue and black bands with a vivid yellow upper section or a navy and red set, and a gold-printed or white type. Erasers are white, wrapped in blue paper sleeves with a black and red print. Technical pens and leads come in clean tin boxes or plastic cases, coloured in blue, black and white, with a simple sans-serif type. The look is functional, German, tidy and classroom-like, with tidy contrast bands and a flat, smooth lacquer.'),
('Faber-Castell', 'Europe', '19th century-present', 'stationery', 'In the style of Faber-Castell, pencils and colouring sets with a deep dark-green lacquer finish and gold hot-foil stamped lettering and a double-knight emblem. The barrels are hexagonal or round, with a smoothly dipped end in colour and fine grading marks. Tins and boxes are dark green, dark brown or black with gold lettering, with rows of pencils and pastels aligned in gradients. Luxury writing tools come in polished wood with silver or platinum-plated fittings and knurled details. Mood is classic, scholarly, European and craftsmanlike.'),
('Pelikan', 'Europe', '1930s-50s', 'writing instruments', 'In the style of Pelikan, piston-filling fountain pens with a plump cigar-shaped barrel and cap in celluloid or resin. Classic models have a black cap, a translucent ink window and a green striped, tortoiseshell or black body with gold-coloured trim bands. The cap clip is a slender arrow or a bird-shaped tail, and a gold nib bears fine engraved scrollwork. Later versions appear in bright blue, red and white stripes, along with a rounded, thick body. Inks and boxes use deep green and white, with a pelican and chicks emblem. The look is tidy, German, sturdy and luminous.'),
('Montblanc', 'Europe', '1920s-present', 'writing instruments', 'In the style of Montblanc, massive cigar-shaped fountain pens in polished black precious resin with gold-plated rings, a clip and a large two-tone gold nib. The cap is topped with a white six-pointed star shaped like a snow-capped peak, rounded with a domed shape. Barrels are glossy and deep black, with thin gold rings and engraved numbers. Special editions use striped, marbled or burgundy resin. Cases are black leather with a gold emblem, and notebooks and cufflinks follow the same dark and gold luxury. The mood is stately, confident, executive and understated.'),
('Parker Pens', 'North America', '1940s-50s', 'writing instruments', 'In the style of Parker Pens, streamlined fountain pens and ballpoints with a cylindrical, slightly tapered body. Barrels come in glossy burgundy, blue, black or grey with a fine herringbone, vertical grooves or smooth finish. Caps are brushed stainless steel or gold-filled, with a ribbed texture, and a distinctive arrow-shaped clip that stands proud from the cap. A hooded nib is covered by a smooth tapered shield. Trim is thin chrome or gold bands. The mood is mid-century post-war optimism, with sleek, bullet-like forms and good engineering.'),
('Waterman Pens', 'Europe', '1920s-50s', 'writing instruments', 'In the style of Waterman Pens, elegant fountain pens with slim, rounded barrels and caps in black hard rubber, red ripple pattern, or sterling silver filigree overlay. Ripple designs swirl in mottled red and black, and metal overlays show Art Deco geometric grooves, basketweave and lacy filigree. Pens have thin gold-filled bands, a lever filler on the side, a gently arched clip and a flexible gold nib with fine ornament. Colours are warm, rich and aged, with an orange-red sheen. Boxes are lined with velvet. The mood is refined, Edwardian and Art Deco.'),
('Sheaffer Pens', 'North America', '1930s-50s', 'writing instruments', 'In the style of Sheaffer Pens, bulbous, torpedo-shaped fountain pens in glossy striped celluloid colours, such as pearl and jade green, marine black, grey, burgundy and blue. The cap has a thin white-dot emblem on top and a slim chrome or gold trim ring. A distinctive inlaid white dot sits at the top of the cap, and a long nib is gold with a triangular or hooded shape. Clips are straight, flat and sleek, with a spear or tapering form. Barrels are smooth, rounded, streamlined and glossy, in an Art Deco and early jet-age manner.'),
('Pilot Namiki Maki-e Pens', 'East Asia', '1920s-present', 'writing instruments', 'In the style of Pilot Namiki Maki-e Pens, slim lacquered fountain pens with a glossy black or red urushi ground decorated with delicate gold, silver and coloured powder. Designs show tiny cranes, dragonflies, bamboo, plum blossom, waves and mountains in raised, sparkling gold with soft gradations, often covering the whole barrel and cap. The metal nib is gold, with fine engraved scrolls, and fittings are gold or rhodium. The forms are smooth and elegant, with rounded ends. The pen comes in a box of paulownia wood. The mood is quiet luxury, patient craft and nature-inspired.'),
('Uni Mitsubishi Pencil', 'East Asia', '1950s-present', 'stationery', 'In the style of Uni Mitsubishi Pencil, premium graphite pencils with a deep, mirror-like glossy paint finish in dark burgundy or black, with a single gold foil stamped line of tiny type and a slim gold band. Hexagonal barrels are smooth and perfectly even, ending in a clean flat end with a thin ferrule. Boxes are slim and rectangular, with a window, in dark maroon, black or silver. Pens such as pure-grey capped markers appear in clear transparent bodies with coloured grips. The overall mood is quiet, luxurious, Japanese, simple and meticulously made.'),
('Lamy', 'Europe', '1960s-80s', 'writing instruments', 'In the style of Lamy, modernist pens with a simple, functional design in matte black plastic and brushed stainless steel. Barrels are straight cylinders with a faceted triangular grip section, and a thin sprung wire clip extends along the cap as a single flat bar. Surfaces are matte and fine-grained, with only small dots or ink windows. Colours are black, charcoal grey, white, and later bright red, yellow and blue. The tip of the nib is silvery steel, clean and plain. Boxes are minimal. The mood is German industrial design, school-like and elegantly austere.'),
('Cross Pens', 'North America', '1940s-present', 'writing instruments', 'In the style of Cross Pens, slim cylindrical pens and pencils with a clean, classical shape and a smooth finish in chrome, brushed gold-filled or lacquer. The barrel carries a tight diamond-patterned engraving or fine ridged stripes and a straight narrow clip with a tiny ball tip. Rings are thin and the profile is a perfectly even tube with a rounded cap that twists at the top. Colours are silver, gold, black and deep blue. The cases are leather or velvet lined. The mood is business-like, classic, restrained, and subtly formal.'),
('Winsor and Newton', 'Europe', '19th century-present', 'art supplies', 'In the style of Winsor and Newton, artists colours in small metal tubes and wooden boxes with a classic, scholarly look. Tubes are silver or coloured foil with black or white lettering, a cream label and a bold red or black band, with the colour name and swatch. Mahogany or black japanned tin watercolour boxes hold rows of half pans in brilliant pigment, with a lift-out tray and a brass catch. Brushes have long, tapered wooden handles in red, black or natural, with a gleaming crimped ferrule. Designs use dense serif type and ornate Victorian borders, giving a studious and traditional feeling.'),
('Caran d Ache', 'Europe', '1930s-present', 'art supplies', 'In the style of Caran d Ache, coloured pencils and crayons in metal tins with a vivid, saturated and carefully graded spectrum. Pencils are hexagonal with a glossy deep colour matching the lead, a white or gold imprint and a smooth round end dipped in the same colour. Tins are slim, in silver, red or royal blue with a hinged lid, neat rows and a bright mountain or lettering emblem. Writing instruments include hexagonal metal pens in glossy lacquer with a crisp chiselled look. Colours are intense, with strong reds, cyans and yellows, and a pristine, orderly, Swiss feel.'),
('Steinway and Sons', 'North America', '1880s-present', 'musical instruments', 'In the style of Steinway and Sons, concert grand pianos in a glossy black polished lacquer, with a mirror-like curved rim, sweeping bentside and a large raised lid. The legs are sturdy, round and tapered, ending in brass casters, with a lyre-shaped pedal assembly in gold-toned metal beneath. A gilt cast-iron plate shows within, the name is in gold lettering on the fallboard and keys are white with ebony sharps. Decoration is elegant and restrained. Alternate finishes include dark mahogany or burl walnut. The mood is grand, opulent, formal and concert-hall dignified.'),
('Bosendorfer', 'Europe', '19th century-present', 'musical instruments', 'In the style of Bosendorfer, Viennese concert grand pianos with a distinctive case in glossy black or rich warm wood, such as walnut, cherry or maple, with a longer, extended bass end of extra black keys. The rim is slender and curved, with a refined and quiet profile. Legs are carved and tapering, and the music desk is lace-like with a shallow ornate shape. Edging is thin, and trim in gold or brass is tasteful. Some models have elaborate inlay, gilt carving and painted panels in the Baroque and Secession manner. The mood is aristocratic, warm, ornate and old-world.'),
('Selmer Paris', 'Europe', '1930s-50s', 'musical instruments', 'In the style of Selmer Paris, saxophones and clarinets with an elegant curved brass body finished in lacquer, with a warm honey-gold hue, or silver plating. Keys are neatly arranged, pearly rounded buttons with fine engraving on the bell showing swirling floral scrollwork in the Art Deco manner. The neck is long and gently bent, and bells are wide and flared. Tone-hole rims are finely drawn and key guards are tidy. Cases are black and plush-lined. The mood is jazz-age, Parisian, tidy and rich in warm gold.'),
('Taylor Guitars', 'North America', '1990s-present', 'musical instruments', 'In the style of Taylor Guitars, acoustic guitars with a modern, clean, sleek look. Bodies are slim with a cutaway, bright natural-coloured spruce tops, and sides and back in sapele, rosewood or koa with fine grain. Finishes are thin, glossy and flawless, and the edges have rounded bevels, especially at the lower arm. Inlays are minimal, such as slim abalone or ebony markers in an angular, elegant shape, with thin bindings and a rosette ring of dark inlay. Headstocks have a tapered, smooth curve with an engraved logo. The mood is precise, bright, contemporary, and carefully finished.'),
('Martin Guitars', 'North America', '1930s-present', 'musical instruments', 'In the style of Martin Guitars, classic dreadnought acoustic guitars with a large, rounded rectangular body, a warm golden spruce top that ages to amber, and rosewood or mahogany sides and back. Bindings are cream or ivoroid and dark herringbone purfling rings the top. The soundhole is circled with fine rosette rings, and the pickguard is tortoiseshell. Fretboards are dark ebony with simple pearl dots or snowflake inlays on premium models. The headstock is squared with an inlaid pearl logo. The finish is satin or gloss. The mood is traditional, folksy and rootsy.'),
('Gretsch Guitars', 'North America', '1950s-60s', 'musical instruments', 'In the style of Gretsch Guitars, large hollow-body electric guitars with a flamboyant orange or deep red-brown lacquer finish, a gold-plated pickguard and bold decoration. Details include twin cutaways, gold sparkle or white binding, a G-shaped brand cutout on the body, a large V-shaped tailpiece in gold, and a tall headstock with a wide shape. Hardware is flashy, with chrome, gold, thumbscrew plates and tall knobs. Pickups sit in gold-plated surrounds. Alternate finishes include white falcon pearl, two-tone green and sparkle. The mood is rock and roll, showy, rockabilly and retro.'),
('Rickenbacker', 'North America', '1960s', 'musical instruments', 'In the style of Rickenbacker, electric guitars and basses with a distinctive thin semi-acoustic body, with a glossy red, fireglo sunburst, jetglo black, mapleglo natural or white finish and white plastic binding. Features include a slotted headstock, a sharply pointed cutaway horn on one side, a stylish chrome bridge cover, triangular fretboard inlays, a chrome toaster-shaped pickup and a white pickguard with a tapered contour. Some models show a rounded cutaway with a curvy body edge and a rear control panel with tiny knobs. The mood is jangly, mod, 1960s British Invasion and psychedelic.'),
('Bechstein', 'Europe', '19th century-present', 'musical instruments', 'In the style of Bechstein, concert and salon grand pianos in glossy ebony black, with a classic Germanic design. Curved cases have a sharply defined rim, bold Jugendstil carved legs or plain tapering legs, and a gold-leaf painted decoration on historic art cases showing Neo-classical or Art Nouveau scenes in muted colours. The lid is large and the music desk is carved. Gold-toned lettering marks the fallboard. Alternative woods include rosewood, walnut and burr maple with warm mellow tones. The mood is cultured, stately, ornate, and drawing-room elegant.'),
('Yamaha Pianos', 'East Asia', '1960s-present', 'musical instruments', 'In the style of Yamaha Pianos, grand and upright pianos in a flawless gloss black polyester finish, with smooth, crisp straight lines and uniform surfaces. Grand pianos have a simple curved rim, a clean music desk, square or rounded legs and a gold or silver fallboard emblem with three crossed tuning forks. Uprights are boxy, with neat panels, a fold-down lid and plain brass pedals. Alternative finishes include satin ebony, polished mahogany, walnut and white. The mood is modern, tidy, dependable, schoolroom and conservatoire, with perfect reflections.'),
('Bauhaus Weimar Workshops', 'Europe', '1919-1925', 'design', 'In the style of Bauhaus Weimar Workshops, expressionist and early constructivist objects made in wood, metal, glass and textile. Furniture is built from stacked cubes, slats and planes in primary red, yellow, blue, black and white, with bold geometric shapes such as circle, square and triangle. Tapestries are abstract compositions of rectangles and stripes in saturated, earthy reds, blues, ochres and black. Ceramics and teapots are rounded and cylindrical in nickel or plain glaze. Posters use heavy sans-serif lettering, stacked on grids with diagonal bars. The mood is utopian, experimental, geometric and artisan.'),
('Vkhutemas Workshops', 'Russia and Eastern Europe', '1920s', 'design', 'In the style of Vkhutemas Workshops, constructivist posters, furniture and architectural models in stark geometry. Colours are red, black, white and cream, with occasional yellow and grey. Compositions place diagonals, circles, wedges and sharp bars at dynamic angles, with heavy angular sans-serif lettering and photomontage. Furniture is made of thin plywood, tubing and simple planes, folded or foldable with exposed joints. Architectural models show stacked boxes, cantilevers, cylinders and glass walls. Prints are flat and high-contrast, with rough printed textures. The mood is revolutionary, industrial, energetic and abstract.'),
('De Stijl Workshops', 'Europe', '1917-1930s', 'design', 'In the style of De Stijl Workshops, furniture, posters and interiors built from straight horizontal and vertical lines and rectangles. Palette is limited to pure red, yellow and blue, together with white, black and grey, set in flat colour blocks with thick black dividing lines. Chairs are assembled from slim rails and flat panels that overlap and project at their ends, painted in contrasting colours with exposed ends. Rooms show asymmetrical balance and open planes. Lettering is blocky and constructed from squares. The mood is strict, rational, abstract and playful in its colour.'),
('Omega Workshops', 'Europe', '1913-1919', 'design', 'In the style of Omega Workshops, hand-painted furniture, ceramics and textiles with bold, loose, post-impressionist decoration. Brush marks are visible, with abstract blobs, stripes, spirals and stylised flowers or figures in strong colours such as mustard, magenta, turquoise, orange, lilac, green and black. Fabrics and screens use irregular, asymmetrical compositions with an unfinished hand-drawn quality. Tables and chairs are plain and painted all over in dense patterns. Pottery has a thick opaque glaze with spots and swirls. The mood is cheerful, avant-garde, amateurish by design and bohemian.'),
('Guild of Handicraft', 'Europe', '1888-1908', 'decorative arts', 'In the style of Guild of Handicraft, Arts and Crafts silver, enamel and jewellery in a hand-wrought manner. Hammered silver bowls, tankards and jewellery show visible tool marks, curved handles, wire twists and loose, flowing Art Nouveau lines. Peacocks, ships, tendrils, stylised flowers and hearts are set with cabochon moonstones, opals, turquoise or blister pearls and translucent coloured enamel in green, blue and rose. Items look soft and slightly irregular, with a matte, pale silver surface and delicate suspended drops. The mood is romantic, medieval-influenced, simple and craftsmanlike.'),
('Shaker Mount Lebanon', 'North America', '19th century', 'furniture', 'In the style of Shaker Mount Lebanon, plain and sturdy furniture and objects made from pine, maple and cherry with no ornament. Chairs have slender turned posts, ladder backs with arching slats, woven tape or rush seats and tapered legs. Tables, cupboards and drawers show clean flat panels, simple pegs, finger joints, wooden knobs and a smooth surface. Colours are muted milk-paint tones, namely barn red, mustard yellow, olive green, blue and the natural honey wood. Oval pantry boxes use swallowtail joints and copper tacks. Rooms have pegboards that hold chairs on the wall. The mood is serene, orderly, utilitarian and pure.'),
('Amish Quilt Workshops', 'North America', 'Late 19th-early 20th century', 'textiles', 'In the style of Amish Quilt Workshops, quilts made with large simple geometric blocks of solid-colour wool or cotton, in deep, saturated tones. Typical palette includes black, plum purple, royal blue, forest green, wine red, brown and dusty rose, with no printed patterns. Designs include a centre diamond, bars, sunshine and shadow, and nine-patch squares, framed by wide borders and bound in contrasting colour. Fine, dense quilting stitches create feathers, wreaths, cables and grids in the dark areas, giving a subtle relief. The mood is bold, austere, modern-looking and graphic.'),
('Gzhel Porcelain', 'Russia and Eastern Europe', '18th century-present', 'ceramics', 'In the style of Gzhel Porcelain, white glazed porcelain and earthenware painted only in cobalt blue brushwork, in graduated tones from pale sky blue to deep navy. Decorations are floral sprigs, leaves, swirling petals, birds, scalloped rims and simple landscapes, painted freely with a wide brush in a single stroke style. Forms include teapots, vases, figurines of animals, tea sets and samovar-like shapes, with rounded and plump outlines. The white ground is bright and glossy, and the blue appears washed and flowing. The mood is folk-like, cheerful, fresh and quintessentially Russian.'),
('Lomonosov Imperial Porcelain Factory', 'Russia and Eastern Europe', '18th-20th century', 'ceramics', 'In the style of Lomonosov Imperial Porcelain Factory, bright white porcelain in two manners. The imperial manner shows gilded rims, cobalt blue nets, a mesh pattern of dark blue lattice with gold dots and rich floral reserves in naturalistic colours. The revolutionary manner of the 1920s shows suprematist and propaganda designs with sharp red, black and white geometry, hammers, stars, workers and slogans in stencilled Cyrillic lettering. Figurines are lively and glossy. Forms are fine, thin and delicate in the imperial style, and flat plates in the later style are filled by boldly angular designs.'),
('Palekh Lacquer Miniatures', 'Russia and Eastern Europe', '1920s-present', 'decorative arts', 'In the style of Palekh Lacquer Miniatures, black glossy papier-mache boxes, brooches and panels painted in egg tempera with fairy tale scenes. Elongated, elegant figures with small heads and graceful curved poses walk through stylised hills, towers, trees and onion domes. Palette is bright red, gold, emerald green, white and orange against deep lustrous black, with fine gold highlights outlining draperies and ornamental borders of tiny patterns. The inside lid is red lacquer. Details are miniature and precise, as in an icon, and the mood is mythical, jewel-like and storybook.'),
('Talavera Uriarte', 'Latin America', '17th century-present', 'ceramics', 'In the style of Talavera Uriarte, tin-glazed earthenware with a thick, glossy milky white glaze and hand-painted decoration in cobalt blue, with accents of yellow, green, orange, black and sometimes turquoise. Patterns are flowers, vines, leaves, birds, rosettes and geometric borders, painted freely with a loose touch and a slightly raised, shiny paint. Plates, jars, jugs, basins and wall tiles are rounded and weighty, with visible pinholes and small unevenness in the glaze. Tiles combine into large courtyard panels in blue and white. The mood is Mexican colonial, rustic, festive and handmade.'),
('Hereke Imperial Carpet Factory', 'Middle East', '19th century', 'textiles', 'In the style of Hereke Imperial Carpet Factory, exceptionally fine silk and wool carpets with a very high knot density, glossy silk pile and sharp, minute detail. Designs show central medallions, prayer arches, Ottoman tulips, carnations, hyacinths, curling saz leaves and rosettes in a courtly arrangement, with metal thread in gold and silver for added shimmer. Colours are ivory, turquoise, rose, deep sapphire, emerald and ruby. Borders are narrow and multiple, with perfectly even geometry and soft elegant curves. The mood is imperial, lustrous, exquisite and delicate.'),
('Batik Danar Hadi', 'Southeast Asia', '20th century-present', 'textiles', 'In the style of Batik Danar Hadi, Javanese hand-drawn wax-resist cotton and silk cloths in earthy palette. Colours include soga brown, indigo blue, cream and black, with occasional red and gold. Patterns are classical, such as diagonal parang bands of interlocking S shapes, kawung circles in a quatrefoil grid, truntum small flowers, and sido mukti cartouches with birds and wings. Fine dotted lines and wavering white outlines from hand-applied wax form the delicate work. Cloth shows crackle veins where the wax cracked, and tonal depth. The mood is courtly, rhythmic, refined and heritage-rich.'),
('Bat Trang Ceramic Kilns', 'Southeast Asia', 'Traditional-present', 'ceramics', 'In the style of Bat Trang Ceramic Kilns, Vietnamese stoneware and porcelain in cream-white glaze with blue underglaze painting, as well as brown, green and iron-brown glazes. Decoration shows lotus flowers, chrysanthemums, dragons, bamboo, birds, wave scrolls and geometric key-fret borders, brushed in free, flowing cobalt blue or incised in clay. Forms are tall vases with swelling shoulders, jars with lids, bowls, plates and teapots, with thick walls and a slightly uneven hand-thrown quality. The crackled ivory glaze is soft and subtle, and the mood is gentle, rustic, and unpretentious.'),
('New Zealand Maori Arts and Crafts Institute', 'Oceania', '20th century-present', 'carving', 'In the style of New Zealand Maori Arts and Crafts Institute, ornately carved wooden meeting house posts, panels and canoe prows covered in dense curved relief. Figures have large heads with wide eyes inlaid with paua shell, protruding tongues, three-fingered hands and bodies decorated with spirals, interlocked koru curves and notched ridges. Wood is dark, often totara, polished brown, with red ochre pigment and details of white and black. Lattice panels and woven reed work, such as tukutuku panels in diagonal patterns, fill the spaces between the posts. The mood is ancestral, powerful, sculptural and rhythmic, with continuous flowing curves.'),
]
